      "question": "What does HttpOnly prevent, and why is a permitted Origin checked separately?",
      "request_id": "1740bea3-d9ce-4091-a9c5-d1d3f7d56bb7",
      "conversation_id": "conversation-9dc3f19e-e5ae-4a8d-87ef-5986c60be35b",
      "student_message_id": "message-27ac0c4f-779d-4a3d-b6a7-0d7ae21d9b9c",
      "response_message_id": "message-67fe7711-fc57-4f08-babf-7fff01258b2f",
      "response_to_message_id": "message-27ac0c4f-779d-4a3d-b6a7-0d7ae21d9b9c",
      "action": "answer",
      "response": "HttpOnly prevents JavaScript from reading a cookie through `document.cookie`; it does not prevent every action an injected script could perform. A permitted Origin is checked separately because browsers can automatically attach cookies to requests, so cookie authentication alone does not establish that a state-changing request was intended by the user. In the course example, state-changing requests therefore require a permitted Origin.\n\nCheck: how would you distinguish the protection provided by HttpOnly from the protection provided by an Origin check?"
    }
